WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.290
Every day people around the world use one-dollar bills.

2
00:00:09.291 --> 00:00:15.580
The U.S. government prints billions of them every year!

3
00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:20.302
People sometimes call these bills "paper money."

4
00:00:20.303 --> 00:00:24.030
But bills are not really made of paper.

5
00:00:24.031 --> 00:00:27.546
They are printed on special cloth.

6
00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:36.362
Seventy-five percent of the cloth is cotton—just like the cotton in your favorite jeans!

7
00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:39.210
The rest of the cloth is linen.

8
00:00:39.211 --> 00:00:45.628
This cloth is much stronger than paper.

9
00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:50.274
Oh, and the bills' ink is special too.

10
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:52.988
It doesn't wash off!

11
00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:56.546
The front of the bill has black ink.

12
00:00:56.547 --> 00:01:00.898
But the back of the bill is printed in green.

13
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:04.432
Green fades less quickly than other colors.

14
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:11.192
This green ink gives all U.S. dollars a nickname—greenbacks!

15
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:14.531
Dollar bills are very sturdy.

16
00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:18.001
But they get passed from hand to hand.

17
00:01:18.002 --> 00:01:22.330
They're folded in wallets and shoved into pockets.

18
00:01:22.331 --> 00:01:25.235
They get torn and wet.

19
00:01:25.236 --> 00:01:27.992
Over time they wear out.

20
00:01:27.993 --> 00:01:35.048
An average dollar bill lasts less than six years.

21
00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.895
So the U.S. government has to replace worn-out bills.

22
00:01:39.896 --> 00:01:44.151
They print new bills to replace the old ones.

23
00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:48.724
The new bills go to banks for people to use.

24
00:01:48.725 --> 00:01:54.496
Banks collect the old bills and send them back to the government.

25
00:01:54.497 --> 00:02:02.306
Workers shred the old bills to destroy them.

26
00:02:02.307 --> 00:02:07.963
Yep, that's right. Money gets destroyed!

27
00:02:07.964 --> 00:02:14.574
In fact, the United States shreds about ten billion dollars a year.

28
00:02:14.575 --> 00:02:19.814
That's enough waste to fill 750 dump trucks!

29
00:02:19.815 --> 00:02:24.472
In the past this waste got dumped in a landfill.

30
00:02:24.473 --> 00:02:30.298
But today getting rid of greenbacks is a much greener process.

31
00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:34.824
The government recycles a lot of old paper money.

32
00:02:34.825 --> 00:02:38.727
Some shredded money goes into roofing material.

33
00:02:38.728 --> 00:02:42.041
Some goes into home insulation.

34
00:02:42.042 --> 00:02:48.888
Other bills become fuel for power plants to make electricity for homes.

35
00:02:48.889 --> 00:02:54.957
Oh, and some paper money becomes filling for mattresses.

36
00:02:54.958 --> 00:02:59.426
So you could even be sleeping on money!

37
00:02:59.427 --> 00:03:03.714
Other bills are sent to farms that use them for compost.

38
00:03:03.715 --> 00:03:10.636
I bet the soil is very rich in those gardens!

39
00:03:10.637 --> 00:03:17.868
I usually spend my dollar bills as soon as I receive them.

40
00:03:17.869 --> 00:03:26.030
But I didn't know that dollar bills have such a hard life!

